Sval: content-classifier features whose list ids come from a pref

A feature may name a pref holding its list ids. Its engine is built from the
pref's value and rebuilt when the pref changes. Sval's `ads` feature takes
the lists of the user's blocking level this way; Mozilla's features keep
their compiled ids. `allowed-domains` is an exception-only feature built
from the user's allowed domains, listed last in the engines prefs so it
excepts every list before it. Its exceptions beat an important block, as
uBlock Origin lets `@@...$important` beat `$important`: adblock-rust files
such exceptions with the others, so after an important block the service
still consults a feature marked mOverridesImportant and counts its exception
as important.

A rebuild whose fetches settle after a newer rebuild's has its engine
installed but never made active, since only the newest call populates the
active lists; Sval's lists, read from disk, settle later than Mozilla's. Such a
rebuild populates the lists again from the newest call's snapshot. Under the
testing pref, the rebuild's notification names the features it installed,
so a test can wait for its own rebuild.

The engines prefs for normal and private windows are written together, and
a pass per write would build a newly active feature once per write, since
its first build has not installed an engine yet; one pass runs after the
turn's writes.

A block reports the matching feature's name as the table to
nsIChannelClassifierService listeners, which is how Sval counts ads apart from
trackers in the protection panel.

kFeatures is a compiled table read only by this file, so no chrome API can
add a feature or change where its lists come from.

